# TASK-260715-sbrrp7 reviewer verdict

Status: CHANGES REQUESTED.

## Passing evidence

- make credential-free-validate LEGACY_ROOT=/Users/iv/Developer/relux-proxy: exit 0.
- The gate passed pinned relay bootstrap, exact scheme regression fixtures, deterministic generation, active/deferred graph checks, unsigned Debug/Release host and provider builds, entitlement/embedding/linkage checks, Swift tests and Release build, native packaging, relay smoke, and isolated legacy tests/Release build.
- Signing-required and deferred-iOS lanes were explicitly reported NOT RUN with reasons.
- sh -n, ShellCheck, workflow YAML parsing, git diff --check, and task-board validate: exit 0.

## Blocking finding

The macOS pull-request job cannot bootstrap Mise as checked in. The workflow uses runs-on: macos-15 but supplies sha256 a945255eb432b900d9db1eaccba14e5ef99c719a5d0ad1a983abc00b2f17388f, which is the mise-v2026.3.10-macos-x64 binary checksum. GitHub currently maps macos-15 to arm64 (actions/runner-images Available Images table). The pinned jdx/mise-action commit derives its target as macos-${process.arch}, downloads the arm64 asset on that runner, and compares the installed binary to the explicit sha256 input. Mise v2026.3.10 publishes arm64 binary checksum c7a0eb1035de974b42d36b69c4b55b836c06b455b990dd6ac530aaf05d4a8a17. Therefore the action will fail with a SHA256 mismatch before invoking make credential-free-validate.

Primary evidence:
- https://github.com/actions/runner-images#available-images
- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/jdx/mise-action/3c2e0cf82a5b2e5249f0d3635a4d83d0ae861518/src/index.ts
- https://github.com/jdx/mise/releases/download/v2026.3.10/SHASUMS256.txt

## Required rework

Use the arm64 raw-binary checksum for runs-on: macos-15, or explicitly select an x64 runner label consistent with the current checksum. Update the contract test and documentation so runner architecture and checksum cannot drift independently, then rerun the same local gate and static validation. No other blocking findings were observed.
